    <div class="art-content" style="max-width:760px;margin:0 auto" data-r>
      <h2>Why we exist</h2>
      <p>Building management is one of the most consequential, least understood corners of the built environment. It decides how much energy a building wastes, how comfortable it is to occupy, and how much its owners pay in maintenance for decades. Yet the whole field sits behind a wall of jargon, proprietary systems, and partner-only knowledge.</p>
      <p>We think that's wrong. <strong>Hurtwood Foundation is an independent resource</strong> dedicated to explaining - in plain English - how building controls actually work, and how open technology could change everything.</p>

      <h2>What we believe</h2>
      <blockquote>Buildings should be open. So should the systems that run them.</blockquote>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Open beats closed.</strong>Open protol beats closed protocol. Open source beats closed protocol. Shared protocols and open source put control back in the hands of building owners.</li>
        <li><strong>Knowledge should be free.</strong> You shouldn't have to join a partner programme to understand your own building.</li>
        <li><strong>Future-proof, not throwaway.</strong> Spend money on making your building work better, not escaping manufracturer impulsed obsolesence. Good buildings shouldn't need ripping out every time a vendor moves on.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who's behind it</h2>
      <p>Hurtwood Foundation is published by <strong>Hurtwood Ltd</strong>, a UK building-management consultancy and the maker of the Satrofit retrofit range. Hurtwood has spent years bringing legacy systems — Satchwell, Sigma, Staefa — into the open, modern world, in buildings from Bristol City Hall to Kew Gardens.</p>
